  "prose_summary": "Section 4.1 identifies and defines what data is — any collection of facts, numbers, measures, observations, or descriptions of things that convey information — then applies this concept by showing how students collect raw data about favourite games in their class. The section demonstrates that unorganised raw lists are difficult to interpret and introduces tally marks as a practical tool for organising data into a frequency table, where the count of each category is its frequency. It then applies the idea of arranging data in ascending order, using a shoe-size example, to show how ordering makes it easier to read off the largest and smallest values and count specific categories. Students are asked to carry out their own data collection tasks involving favourite games, tree counts along a route, and letter frequencies in newspaper articles, reinforcing the cycle of collecting, organising, and reading data.\n\nSection 4.2 defines pictographs and applies their construction and reading to several real contexts. The section recalls prior knowledge of pictographs from earlier classes and establishes the key structural features: data is represented through pictures or symbols, each carrying a stated scale or key (one symbol equals one or more units). A worked example by Nand Kishor on students' sleep habits demonstrates how to read a pictograph where each symbol represents 10 children, including how to interpret a half-symbol as five children. The section then applies the drawing process through Lakhanpal's absent-students data (one symbol equals one student), and explores how to choose a larger scale when data values are numerous, illustrated by Jarina (one symbol equals five students) and Sangita (one symbol equals ten students, with a half-symbol for five). Key properties are stated: pictographs provide a visual and suggestive representation, support quick comparison of frequencies, and require a clearly stated scale; they become challenging when data is large or values are not exact multiples of the chosen scale.\n\nSection 4.3 identifies bar graphs as a more precise and scalable alternative to pictographs for representing data. The section introduces bar graphs by revisiting Lakhanpal's absent-students data in bar-graph form and explains the structural rules: bars of uniform width, equal spacing between bars, and a scale where the length or height of each bar encodes its frequency. It then applies these principles to a worked example of Delhi vehicular traffic (horizontal bar graph, one unit equals 100 vehicles), showing how to identify the maximum and minimum bars and compute combined intervals. A second example presents the population of India across six decades as a vertical bar graph (one unit equals 10 crores), demonstrating scale selection to handle large numbers. Key properties of bar graphs are summarised: they enable faster frequency comparison than tables, allow glancing identification of the largest and smallest categories, and require a clearly stated scale starting from zero.\n\nSection 4.4 applies the full step-by-step procedure for drawing a bar graph from raw or tabular data. Beginning with Shri Nilesh's sweet-preference frequency table, the section explains drawing two perpendicular axes, labelling categories on the horizontal axis, choosing an appropriate scale (here one unit equals one student), computing bar heights equal to each frequency, and drawing bars of uniform width with equal spacing. A worked example of Smriti's cricket runs across eight matches (one unit equals 10 runs) demonstrates how to select a scale that avoids impractical bar heights when the maximum value is large. A second worked example with Imran's family monthly expenditure on six items (one unit equals two hundred rupees) demonstrates converting expenditure to bar heights by division, and then constructing and reading the completed bar graph. Students then apply the procedure themselves in a series of exercises involving insect counts, railway ticket data, transport frequency tables, dice experiments, cricket statistics, and more.\n\nSection 4.5 explains the artistic and aesthetic considerations that govern effective data presentation and reasons about when design choices aid or hinder accurate communication. The section establishes that scale selection and bar orientation both affect how well a graph fits its intended space and how intuitively it communicates its data. Using the tallest mountain on each continent as an example, it reasons that vertical bars (column graphs) are more intuitive for representing height, while horizontal bars suit lengths parallel to the ground. It then introduces infographics — visually enriched data presentations using engaging imagery — and cautions that artistic choices, such as using triangles of varying widths for mountains, can imply additional information (e.g., that taller mountains are wider) that may mislead. The section closes by emphasising that visual appeal must never come at the cost of accuracy, and that readers of infographics should be alert to potential distortions.",
