  "prose_summary": "Section 5.1 recalls the concepts of multiples and factors and applies them through two engaging games — the Idli-Vada game and Jump Jackpot — to build intuition about common multiples and common factors. In the Idli-Vada game, students say 'idli' for multiples of 3, 'vada' for multiples of 5, and 'idli-vada' for common multiples of both; this playful context motivates the idea of the least common multiple as the first number where both conditions coincide. The Jump Jackpot game then applies the same logic from the other direction: Jumpy must choose a jump size that lands on two treasure numbers simultaneously, which requires the jump size to divide both numbers — that is, to be a common factor. A Venn-diagram-style figure (Fig. 5.1) visually organises multiples of 3 and multiples of 5 into overlapping regions, with common multiples sitting at the intersection. Students are prompted to explore games with other number pairs and to discover that the common multiples of two numbers are the multiples of their LCM, and that the common factors are the factors of their GCF. A shaded table of numbers from 31 to 70 provides a further stimulus for a Math Talk on which cells are multiples of 3, multiples of 4, or both. The section closes with a rich Figure it Out set requiring computation of common multiples, common factors, and reconstruction of Venn diagrams from only the common-multiple region.\n\nSection 5.2 defines prime numbers and composite numbers by reasoning about rectangular arrangements of objects, then identifies the Sieve of Eratosthenes as a systematic method to list all primes up to 100. The opening narrative contrasts Guna (who can arrange 12 figs in multiple rectangles because 12 has more than two factors) with Anshu (who can only arrange 7 figs in a single 1 × 7 or 7 × 1 strip because 7 has exactly two factors). From this, the section defines prime numbers as those with exactly two factors (1 and itself) and composite numbers as those with more than two factors; it additionally states that 1 is neither prime nor composite. The Sieve of Eratosthenes is introduced with step-by-step instructions — circle 2 and strike out its multiples, circle 3 and strike out its multiples, continue — and a Math Talk prompt invites students to reason why the procedure works. A historical note on Eratosthenes situates the method 2,200 years ago, and a sidebar notes that the largest known prime would require 6,500 pages to write. The Figure it Out set following the Sieve asks students to analyse patterns among primes up to 100: identify twin primes, find consecutive composites, classify true/false statements about parity and primality, and find primes whose doubling-plus-one is also prime.\n\nSection 5.3 recalls the treasure-hunting game from section 5.1 and applies it to introduce the concept of co-prime numbers, defining two numbers as co-prime if their only common factor is 1. The section extends the game with a new rule — jump size 1 is forbidden — so that Grumpy must place treasures on numbers that share no common factor greater than 1, making them unreachable by a single jump size. Students check whether pairs such as (15, 39), (4, 15), (18, 29) and (20, 55) are safe (co-prime) or unsafe, building operational understanding of the definition before any formal test. A Math Talk prompt asks students to connect the co-prime property to the Idli-Vada game: when the two game numbers are co-prime, the first common multiple equals their product; when they are not co-prime, it is strictly smaller. A visual Co-prime Art activity presents thread diagrams wound around pegs: when the peg count and thread-gap are co-prime, every peg is visited exactly once before the thread returns to the start; when they share a common factor, only some pegs are visited. Students apply this by predicting and drawing patterns for four peg/gap combinations.\n\nSection 5.4 reasons about the limitations of ad hoc factorisation and then applies prime factorisation as the definitive tool for determining co-primality and divisibility. The opening dialogue between Anshu and Guna exposes Anshu's error: writing 56 = 14 × 4 does not reveal all factors of 56, so a common factor (7) shared with 63 can be missed. This motivates the need for a complete, systematic factorisation. The section defines prime factorisation — expressing a number as a product of primes by repeatedly breaking composite factors — and shows through a tree diagram that 36 can be factorised four different ways yet always yields two 2s and two 3s. A diagram connecting 30 = 2 × 3 × 5 along different multiplication paths illustrates why order does not matter (commutativity and associativity, to be studied formally later). The uniqueness of prime factorisation (up to order) is stated as a remarkable fact without proof. Two applications are then developed: first, checking co-primality by comparing prime factor lists (56 = 2³ × 7 and 63 = 3² × 7 share the factor 7, so they are not co-prime; 80 = 2⁴ × 5 and 63 = 3² × 7 share no prime, so they are co-prime); second, checking divisibility by verifying that the prime factorisation of the potential divisor is included in that of the dividend (168 = 2³ × 3 × 7 is divisible by 12 = 2² × 3; 42 = 2 × 3 × 7 is not divisible by 12 = 2² × 3 because 2 appears only once in 42 but twice in 12).\n\nSection 5.5 reasons about patterns in multiples to derive simple divisibility tests for 10, 5, 2, 4, and 8 without performing long division, and applies each test to a running example. The section opens by noting that finding factors of large numbers by trial is tedious and motivates the search for short-cut rules. For divisibility by 10, the pattern that all multiples of 10 end in 0 is observed and stated; Math Talk prompts invite students to agree or disagree with the general rule. For 5, the last-digit pattern (0 or 5) is derived similarly. For 2, the even last-digit pattern (0, 2, 4, 6, 8) is established. Divisibility by 4 is more subtle: a single last digit is insufficient because 12 and 22 share a last digit yet only 12 is divisible by 4. Students are led to discover through exploration that only the last two digits determine divisibility by 4, and three explicit statements about the equivalence of the last-two-digit rule are presented for Math Talk verification. Divisibility by 8 extends this reasoning to the last three digits; the same three-statement structure is used. The section closes by noting that tests for 3, 6, 7, and 9 will be developed in later classes. The Figure it Out set applies all five rules together: a leap-year problem requires divisibility by 4, 100, and 400; palindrome questions combine divisibility by 4 with digit-reversal constraints; and a culminating problem asks which two of the five divisors (2, 4, 5, 8, 10) it is sufficient to check to certify divisibility by all five.\n\nSection 5.6 applies number-theory concepts from the chapter in open-ended, playful contexts that reward creative mathematical reasoning. The Special Numbers activity presents boxes of four numbers and challenges students to explain, for each number, what property makes it different from the other three — the same number can be special for multiple reasons, and the class activity surfaces different valid arguments for the same number (e.g., 43 is special both as the only prime and as the only non-square). This section is a Math Talk-led exploration with no unique correct answer. A Prime Puzzle challenges students to fill a grid with prime numbers so that the product of each row equals a given number on the right and the product of each column equals a given number at the bottom, applying prime factorisation skills in a two-dimensional constraint-satisfaction setting.",
