  "prose_summary": "Section 6.1 defines the perimeter of any closed polygon as the total boundary distance — the sum of all side lengths — and then recalls the three standard formulas for polygons students have encountered before. For a rectangle, the perimeter equals twice the sum of its length and breadth, a formula derived by recognising that opposite sides are equal (AB = CD, AD = BC), so the four-sided sum collapses to 2 × (l + b). For a square, since all four sides are equal, the perimeter is simply four times the side length. For a triangle, the perimeter is the direct sum of all three sides. Two worked examples anchor the formulas in context: one finds the lace required around a 3 m × 2 m tablecloth, the other computes the distance Usha travels in three rounds of a 75 m square park.\n\nSection 6.1 then extends perimeter reasoning to richer situations. The Matha Pachchi scenario applies and compares perimeters by computing how far Akshi and Toshi run on two concentric rectangular tracks (outer: 70 m × 40 m; inner: 60 m × 30 m), introducing the idea of positional reasoning after a given distance covered. The Estimate and Verify activity asks students to cut irregular paper shapes and physically measure boundary length before computing. The section introduces straight and diagonal units to make perimeter measurement on dot-grid figures precise, noting that diagonal segments are longer than straight segments. Regular polygons are then defined as closed figures with all sides and all angles equal; the perimeter of an equilateral triangle and the general rule (perimeter = number of sides × side length) generalise the formulas already seen for squares. The Split and rejoin task has students cut a 6 cm × 4 cm rectangle and rejoin the two halves in four different configurations, computing the boundary of each arrangement, including a target configuration with perimeter 22 cm. The Making it 'More' or 'Less' investigation asks students to arrange 9 unit squares in connected figures and explore the range of achievable perimeters, distinguishing minimum (compact 3×3 arrangement, 12 units) from maximum (linear 1×9 arrangement, 20 units), and reasoning about how attaching a new square to an existing figure changes the perimeter depending on how many sides become shared.\n\nSection 6.2 recalls that area is the amount of region enclosed by a closed figure and is measured in square units, then applies the formulas (area of rectangle = l × b; area of square = s²) to composite real-life contexts. Two worked examples solve composite subtraction problems: one finds the uncarpeted floor area when a 3 m square carpet is placed in a 5 m × 4 m room, another finds the remaining land after four 4 m square flower beds are placed at the corners of a 12 m × 10 m plot. The section reinforces that regions can be found by subtracting one area from another, or by splitting an irregular shape into rectangles. The tangram activity builds area intuition by having students compare pieces whose areas are equal or related by simple multiples, then count the area of the combined square in terms of the smallest piece. Area estimation on grid paper is formalised with four conventions for counting partial squares. The Let's Explore activity justifies why area is measured in squares (they tile perfectly without gaps or overlaps, unlike circles), then investigates all rectangles of a fixed area of 24 square units to discover that the rectangle approaching a square shape has the smallest perimeter while the most elongated has the largest. Two house-plan problems (Charan's and Sharan's) apply area and perimeter computation to architectural floor plans with several missing dimensions to recover. The Area Maze Puzzles require multi-step reasoning: given areas of sub-rectangles and some dimensions, students must compute missing areas or side lengths.\n\nSection 6.3 develops the area formula for a triangle by building on the rectangle. Students physically cut a rectangle along its diagonal and discover that the two triangles are congruent and each has exactly half the area of the rectangle. The key conceptual step is that this holds even for a triangle (such as triangle ABE) whose apex does not sit directly above a base vertex: the triangle is decomposed into two right triangles (AEF and BEF), each of which is half of a sub-rectangle (AFED and BFEC respectively), so the total area of triangle ABE is half the area of the enclosing rectangle ABCD. This reasoning establishes the general principle that the area of any triangle with base b and corresponding height h equals ½ × b × h, stated implicitly through the cutting argument. Exercises on grid paper require students to compute areas of five given triangular and composite figures by decomposing them into rectangular and triangular sub-regions. The Making it 'More' or 'Less' strand revisits the tension between area and perimeter, and a final Figure it Out block at p.149 applies all area and perimeter ideas to eight problems that mix direct calculation, inverse problems, design challenges, and a logical reasoning question about what happens to perimeter and area when a square is folded and cut.",
