  "prose_summary": "Section 3.1 identifies the need for subdividing standard units of measurement through a motivating real-world situation: Sonu's mother cannot fix a toy because two visually similar screws differ in length by a fraction of a centimetre. The section directs students to place screws on a millimetre-ruled scale and record measurements such as 2 7/10 cm, building the intuition that whole-number centimetre readings are insufficient when differences are small. The dominant act is recognising why smaller units are necessary — the section does not yet introduce formal notation or arithmetic.\n\nSection 3.2 defines the one-tenth as a unit, establishes how fractional lengths are composed, and then applies addition and subtraction of tenth-fractions in context. The pencil-length diagram introduces the mixed number notation (3 4/10 units) and shows that 34 one-tenths equal three full units plus four tenths. The section provides guided worked computation for adding the lower and upper arm lengths (2 7/10 + 3 6/10 = 6 3/10) showing both 'units and tenths' and 'convert to tenths' methods, then extends to subtraction (12 4/10 – 6 7/10 = 5 7/10) with an analogous regrouping argument. Students practice ordering eighth-item lists of tenth-fractions and continuing six numerical sequences that increase or decrease in tenth steps.\n\nSection 3.3 defines the one-hundredth by motivating through the folded-paper problem: a sheet of length 8 9/10 units folded in half gives a length expressible only with hundredths. The section establishes that each one-tenth is split into 10 parts giving 100 parts per unit, and that the length 4 5/10 45/100 may be written as 4 units and 45 one-hundredths. Multiple representations for the same length are shown (wire diagram: 1 1/10 4/100 = 1 14/100 = 114/100). Students then apply addition and subtraction of mixed tenths-and-hundredths quantities using two parallel methods — column-wise place value decomposition (analogous to whole-number column arithmetic) and complete conversion to hundredths — and are invited to notice structural similarity with whole-number addition.\n\nSection 3.4 reasons through why the decimal system splits by powers of ten, then applies that reasoning to introduce decimal point notation and read decimal numbers. The section opens by asking whether a unit could equally be split into 4 or 8 equal parts, and then justifies splitting into 10 by pointing to the role of 10 in the Indian place value system. The place value chain is extended leftward (10,000 → 1000 → 100 → 10 → 1) and then rightward (1 → 1/10 → 1/100 → 1/1000 → 1/10000), naming this the decimal system and noting the Sanskrit-Latin etymology of 'decem/daśha'. The section then applies the place value table to show how the decimal point disambiguates otherwise identical digit strings (705, 70.5, 7.05), defines how to read decimal numbers aloud, and walks through computing 234 tenths = 23.4 using place value decomposition.\n\nSection 3.5 applies decimal notation to real measurement conversions across three systems: length (mm ↔ cm ↔ m), weight (g ↔ kg, mg ↔ g), and currency (paise ↔ rupee). Each conversion is derived from the known integer ratio (1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 rupee = 100 paise) and expressed as a unit fraction leading directly to a decimal: 1 mm = 0.1 cm, 1 cm = 0.01 m, 1 g = 0.001 kg, 1 paisa = 0.01 rupee. Partially worked examples illustrate multi-digit conversions (e.g. 15 cm = 0.15 m; 254 g = 0.254 kg; 75 paise = 0.75 rupee) and students fill blanks in three fill-in-the-blank tables. A Try This prompt invites students to interview family members about historical prices and look for old coins.\n\nSection 3.6 applies decimal numbers to locating them on the number line, comparing their magnitude, and identifying the decimal closest to a given target. The section shows how 1.4 lies between 1 and 2 by subdividing that unit interval into 10 parts and marking the fourth. It then zooms in further to locate three-decimal-place numbers such as 4.185 by successive magnification — a powerful visual that grounds place value spatially. The 'Zero Dilemma' Math Talk establishes that trailing zeros after the decimal point do not change value (0.2 = 0.20 = 0.200) while leading zeros after the decimal point do change value (0.02 ≠ 0.2). Comparison of 6.456 and 6.465 is shown visually on the number line and then by left-to-right digit comparison, mirroring the procedure for whole numbers.\n\nSection 3.7 applies the column-arithmetic procedure for whole numbers directly to decimal addition and subtraction. Opening with a cloth-purchase context (2.7 m + 3.5 m = 6.2 m), the section shows in parallel the fraction form and the decimal column form, making explicit that they compute the same regrouping. A detailed place value expansion for 75.345 + 86.691 = 162.036 is shown column-by-column, followed by compact column form. The Figure it Out exercise includes eight additions and eight subtractions ranging from single-tenths to three-decimal-place numbers, and a Decimal Sequences task where students identify the constant addend or subtrahend in eight arithmetic progressions with decimal steps and write the next three terms.\n\nSection 3.8 reasons about the decimal system through two lenses: real-world measurement disasters caused by decimal errors, and the history of decimal notation. The measurement-disaster vignette describes an Air Canada Boeing 767 that nearly crashed because fuel was loaded in pounds instead of kilograms (a unit confusion compounded by a decimal misread), an Amsterdam housing-benefit overpayment of €188 million due to a cents-for-euros programming error, and medical dosing errors from misreading 0.05 mg as 0.5 mg. The 'Deceptive Decimal Notation' discussion then shows that decimal point notation can be misleading when the base is not ten — cricket overs (base 6) and feet-and-inches (base 12) use a decimal-like separator but are not decimals. The historical aside traces decimal fractions through 8th-century Indian astronomers, al-Uqlīdisī's 950 CE formalisation, and 15th–16th century European notations leading to the modern point.",
