  "prose_summary": "Section 3.1 recalls and identifies the core principle of proportional relationships: two ratios a:b and c:d are proportional if the cross-products are equal (a×d = b×c), or equivalently a/c = b/d. The idli batter example (Viswanath 6:3 vs Puneet 4:2) grounds the definition in a real-world cooking scenario and confirms both mixtures maintain the same 2:1 ratio, making the proportional relationship concrete before formal notation is introduced.\n\nSection 3.2 applies proportional reasoning to map scales. It defines the Representative Fraction (RF) as the ratio of map distance to actual ground distance, using RF 1:60,00,000 as the working example (1 cm on map equals 60 km on ground). Students use a ruler on a printed map of South India to measure distances between city pairs (Bengaluru–Chennai, Mangaluru–Chennai) and convert using the RF. A teacher-guided Map Making Activity directs students to sketch their own classroom to scale (1:50), placing furniture and fixtures at proportionally correct positions using appropriate symbols.\n\nSection 3.3 extends ratio notation beyond two terms and defines multi-term proportionality. Using a four-ingredient spice mix (coriander:chillies:dal:fenugreek :: 8:4:2:1), it shows how all terms scale by the same factor when quantities change proportionally. The general rule a/p = b/q = c/r = d/s formalises the relationship. Two worked examples apply multi-term ratios to mixing paint shades (Red:Blue:White :: 2:3:5, find red and blue for a given volume of white) and cement concrete (cement:sand:gravel :: 1:1.5:3, find total bags for 3 bags of cement).\n\nSection 3.4 solves for individual components when a whole quantity is divided in a given multi-term ratio. It extends the two-term division algorithm — divide the whole by the sum of ratio terms, then multiply each term — to three or more terms, stating the general formula: each part equals x × (its ratio term) / (sum of all ratio terms). Three worked examples cover finding component quantities in 110 units of concrete (ratio 1:1.5:3), finding ml of each paint colour for 50 ml of purple (ratio 2:3:5), and constructing a triangle whose angles are in ratio 1:3:5 (using the angle-sum constraint of 180°).\n\nSection 3.5 applies ratio-based division to pie chart construction. Starting from a grade-distribution table (A:B:C:D:E :: 12:10:8:6:4), it reduces the ratio to simplest form (6:5:4:3:2) using the HCF, then divides 360° proportionally to obtain sector angles (108°, 90°, 72°, 54°, 36°). A step-by-step construction procedure guides students to draw a circle, mark a radius, and measure successive angles with a protractor to complete and colour the pie chart. The section connects data visualisation directly back to the proportional division framework established in section 3.4.\n\nSection 3.6 defines inverse proportions and applies the relationship to a range of word problems. It begins by contrasting inverse with direct proportion: using a speed-time table (walk 5 km/h / 18h, bicycle 15/6, motorcycle 30/3, car 60/1.5), it shows that when one quantity increases by factor n, the other decreases by the same factor n, so their product xy = k (constant distance of 90 km). The formal rule x₁y₁ = x₂y₂ is derived and then applied across six worked examples — workers-and-days road laying, pump-and-hours tank filling, food provisions for expanding school population, and combined work rates (Ram 1hr + Shyam 1.5hr cutting vegetables together, yielding 3/5 hr). The final example introduces unit-work-rate reasoning as a bridge between inverse and direct proportionality, requiring students to identify which relationship governs each sub-step.",
