  "prose_summary": "Section 5.1 (The Balancing Act) reasons about the mean as a balance point and applies this insight to predict how mean and median shift when values are added, removed, or uniformly transformed. The section opens by revisiting mean as arithmetic average and reframes it geometrically: the mean is the unique point on a number line where the total distance of all values to its left equals the total distance to its right. Using dot plots, students verify this balance property for collections of two and three numbers and argue why the centre must be unique — shifting left or right immediately disturbs the equality of distances. The section then explores how the mean responds to adding or removing values: a value greater than the mean pulls it up, a value smaller pulls it down, and removing a value equal to the mean leaves it unchanged. Students investigate whether pairs or triples of values can be added or removed without changing the mean, discovering that symmetric additions around the current mean preserve balance. Next, the section proves algebraically what happens when every value in a dataset is increased or decreased by a fixed constant: the mean shifts by exactly that constant. A similar algebraic argument shows that multiplying every value by a constant scales the mean by the same factor, using the distributive property in both proofs. A parallel treatment covers the median: because the median is the middle value in sorted order, including a value larger than the current median increases it, while a value smaller decreases it. The section then moves to applied problem-solving: two worked problems (finding a smudged weight in a wrestler's record and correcting an over-counted coconut harvest) model how to use the mean formula to recover an unknown from a given average. The sub-section on mean and median with frequencies introduces frequency tables and shows that multiplying each value by its frequency before summing is necessary when data is not listed individually; it also shows how cumulative frequencies allow the median position to be located without writing out all values. The section closes with a spreadsheets sub-topic: students see how a digital spreadsheet is structured (rows, columns, cell references), learn to read a marks table, and learn to write SUM and AVERAGE formulae over row and column ranges, connecting the abstract concept of mean to a practical computational tool.\n\nSection 5.2 (Visualising and Interpreting Data) applies a two-step interpret-and-infer process to several real data visualisations, building skill in reading line graphs, infographics, and time-use strips. The section introduces line graphs as the appropriate choice when data evolves over time, contrasting them with the clustered bar graphs seen in earlier grades. Two worked examples are developed in depth: monthly maximum temperature data for Kerala and Punjab across 2023, and annual satellite-launch counts for India, China, Russia, and worldwide from 2012 to 2024. For each, students first identify what the graph shows (scale, units, colour/marker conventions, time range) and then infer patterns and comparisons — Punjab's wider temperature range versus Kerala's near-constant climate, or the sharp acceleration of worldwide launches after 2020 driven largely by USA. The rainfall line graph for six Indian coastal cities introduces the idea that monthly-average data is itself a derived quantity (averaged over multiple years), prompting discussion of what the data can and cannot tell us. The section then presents an infographic — a choropleth map comparing per-capita rice and wheat consumption across Indian states — illustrating how colour encoding can reveal geographic patterns not visible in a table, and probing what inferences are valid versus speculative. The activity-strip sub-section introduces a novel representation: a 48-box colour-coded strip where each box represents a 30-minute interval across one day, and five acti